r of start (or end?) but the description says \"Apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift\". That means we should process calls in descending order of their start (or end?) to avoid shifting indices. Since we are replacing spans, if we replace from left to right, indices shift. So we process from highest start to lowest start. But we also need to order accepted calls by source start for reporting? The output selected_call_ids should be in order of source start? The requirement says \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So the selected_call_ids should be sorted by start ascending. That is separate from application order.\n\nProcedure:\n1. Input: case dictionary with keys 'tokens' and 'calls'.\n2. Validate required fields: tokens list, calls list. Each call must have call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. Not specified but we assume existence. If missing, skip? But likely always present.\n3. Rank calls: sort by (-score, start, call_id) for selection? The description: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID. Greedily reject any span overlapping an already selected span.\" So we should sort calls in that order (desc score, then ascending start, then ascending call_id). Then iterate, maintaining a set of accepted calls. For each call, check if its span [start, end) overlaps with any already accepted call's span. If not, accept it. Overlap means intervals intersect? Since end is exclusive? Usually start inclusive, end exclusive. Overlap if max(start1, start2) < min(end1, end2). We'll use that.\n4. After selecting accepted calls, sort them by start ascending (source start) to get the order for selected_call_ids output.\n5. For applying replacements: we need to produce the final token list. Start with original tokens. For each accepted call, in order of descending start (right to left), replace tokens[start:end] with the call's replacement_tokens (a list of strings). The replacement may be multiple tokens. So we need to update the tokens list. Since we process from right to left, we don't affect indices of earlier spans.\n6. After all replacements, the final tokens list is the interleaved token stream.\n7. Return {'selected_call_ids': list of call_ids sorted by start, 'tokens': final token list}.\n\nEdge cases: If calls have overlapping spans that are accepted? Not if greedy reject. If a call's replacement_tokens is empty? Possibly, then we delete that span. That's fine.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) returning a dict.\n\nNow produce the implementation string.\n\nNote: The output schema requires exactly \"selected_call_ids\" and \"tokens\". No additional keys.\n\nImplementation will be Python code. We'll write it inside a function solve(case). Must not mutate case, so we make a copy of tokens.\n\nLet's produce the string with proper escaping for JSON.\n\nAlso, fixture_payload shows nonce field but we ignore.\n\nWrite code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: indexing: start and end as given. Ensure we treat end as exclusive.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case['tokens'][:]  # copy\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # Validate (optional)\n    # Rank candidates\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    accepted = []\n    # Track selected intervals\n    selected_intervals = []  # list of (start, end)\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        # check overlap with any selected interval\n        overlap = False\n        for (ss, ee) in selected_intervals:\n            if max(s, ss) < min(e, ee):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            accepted.append(c)\n            selected_intervals.append((s, e))\n    # Sort accepted by start ascending\n    accepted_sorted = sorted(accepted, key=lambda c: c['start'])\n    # Apply replacements right to left\n    # Sort by start descending\n    apply_order = sorted(accepted, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True)\n    for c in apply_order:\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        repl = c['replacement_tokens']\n        # Replace tokens[s:e] with repl\n        tokens = tokens[:s] + repl + tokens[e:]\n    # Now interleaved tokens\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in accepted_sorted]\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": tokens}\n\nTest with given example? Let's mentally test with first case:\ntokens: t0..t9.\ncalls: a(1,3) score0.8, b(2,5) score0.9, c(7,9) score0.7.\nSorted by score desc: b (0.9), a (0.8), c (0.7). For ties? none.\nSelect b: interval [2,5). selected_intervals = [(2,5)]\nCheck a: [1,3) overlaps with [2,5) because max(1,2)=2 < min(3,5)=3 => overlap, reject.\nCheck c: [7,9) no overlap, accept. selected_intervals = [(2,5),(7,9)].\naccepted: b, c.\nSort by start: b (start2), c (start7). selected_call_ids = ['b','c'].\nApply right to left: order by start desc: c (start7), b (start2).\nApply c: replace tokens[7:9] (t7,t8) with ['<c>'] => tokens becomes t0..t6 + '<c>' + t9 => indices shift? Actually after c, length reduces by 1. Then apply b: replace tokens[2:5] (original t2,t3,t4) with ['<b>']. Since we processed rightmost first, the indices for b are still valid because no earlier replacement affected positions after 2? Wait, after c replacement, the tokens list length changed. The original b start=2, end=5 still refer to original positions. But we replaced c first, which was after b's span, so b's span indices are unchanged because c's span is after b's end. So it's safe. Apply b: replace tokens[2:5] with ['<b>'] => tokens now: t0,t1,'<b>',t5,t6,'<c>',t9? Wait let's simulate:\nOriginal: [t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]\nApply c first: replace indices 7 to 9 (t7,t8) with ['<c>'] => new list: t0,t1,t2,t3,t4,t5,t6,'<c>',t9 (length 9)\nNow apply b: replace indices 2 to 5 (original t2,t3,t4) with ['<b>'] => new list: t0,t1,'<b>',t5,t6,'<c>',t9 (since after removal of t2,t3,t4, the elements shift: after index2, we have the rest). So final tokens: ['t0','t1','<b>','t5','t6','<c>','t9']. That seems correct interleaving: t0,t1,<b>,t5,t6,<c>,t9. Note that original t2,t3,t4 replaced, t7,t8 replaced.\n\nReturn selected_call_ids=['b','c'], tokens = that list.\n\nThat matches expected behavior.\n\nNow ensure output is JSON serializable. Python list of strings and list of strings.\n\nWe need to wrap in implementation string.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nMake sure to escape properly: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nImplementation string in JSON.\n\nLet's write the Python code as a string.\n\nWe'll also include comments? Not necessary but can be minimal. Keep it simple.\n\nPotential issues: if case has missing fields, but per contract we assume valid.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case['tokens'][:]\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # sort by descending score, then start, then call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    accepted = []\n    intervals = []\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        overlap = False\n        for ss, ee in intervals:\n            if max(s, ss) < min(e, ee):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            accepted.append(c)\n            intervals.append((s, e))\n    # sort accepted by start for reporting\n    accepted_by_start = sorted(accepted, key=lambda c: c['start'])\n    # apply replacements from right to left\n    apply_order = sorted(accepted, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True)\n    for c in apply_order:\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        repl = c['replacement_tokens']\n        tokens = tokens[:s] + repl + tokens[e:]\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in accepted_by_start]\n    return {'selected_call_ids': selected_call_ids, 'tokens': tokens}\n\nCheck that we didn't mutate case: we used case['tokens'][:] to copy.\n\nNow we need to